### <img src="https://flagcdn.com/no.svg" width="32" alt="Norway" style={{display: 'inline', verticalAlign: 'middle', position: 'relative', top: '-2px', marginRight: '8px', border: '1px solid #d1d5db', borderRadius: '2px'}} />Norway

Norway demonstrates extraordinary institutional resilience through unprecedented accountability responses to Epstein-related scandals. PM's public criticism of Crown Princess and former PM, corruption investigation opening against former PM Jagland, and ambassador resignation show democratic mechanisms functioning robustly at highest levels. Economic institutions maintain stability through routine corporate and sovereign wealth fund activities. Defense posture continues established modernization trajectory with routine threat assessments. US diplomatic relationship maintains operational stability despite continued political friction. Government's accountability response to elite establishment scandals represents institutional strength operating under pressure.

**Key developments:**

- **Domestic:** PM Jonas Gahr Støre publicly stated that Crown Princess Mette-Marit and former PM Thorbjørn Jagland showed 'poor judgment' in their contacts with Jeffrey Epstein. International media described this as unprecedented - no previous example exists of a Norwegian PM criticizing royal family members in this manner. Initially rejected independent investigation but eventually agreed to parliamentary review.
- **Domestic:** Økokrim launched gross corruption investigation against former PM and Nobel Committee chair Thorbjørn Jagland for contacts with Jeffrey Epstein while serving in high-level positions 2016-2018. Foreign Minister announced Norway will propose lifting Jagland's diplomatic immunity to Europarådet. Investigation represents accountability mechanisms targeting former highest officials.
- **Diplomatic:** Ambassador Mona Juul resigned from her Jordan position after revelations about her contacts with Jeffrey Epstein. Foreign Minister Barth Eide called it a 'necessary decision' after conversations during the week. UD continues investigating her Epstein contacts and their potential impact on diplomatic work.
- **Diplomatic:** PM Støre sent message to President Trump urging Russia to extend bombing pause in ongoing conflict. At Arctic Frontiers conference, Støre also discussed with EU officials Norway's opposition to Arctic oil moratorium, arguing all Norwegian LNG to EU comes from Arctic regions.
- **Security:** E-tjenesten, PST and NSM released annual threat assessments identifying Russia and China as primary threats. Reports highlight sabotage risks, digital vulnerabilities, and concerns about US pressure on Greenland potentially benefiting Moscow and Beijing. Ministers emphasized this as the most serious security situation since WWII.
- **Unexpected:** PM delivers unprecedented public criticism of Crown Princess over Epstein contacts.

<Accordion title="Other Stories">
- **Crown Princess Mette-Marit apologizes for Epstein contacts amid royal family crisis** — Crown Princess Mette-Marit issued public apology for her extensive email contacts with convicted sex offender Jeffrey Epstein, expressing regret to King Harald and Queen Sonja. Crisis compounds with her son Marius Borg Høiby's rape trial. King Harald and Queen Sonja attended Winter Olympics amid the scandal. *([apnews.com](https://theguardian.com/world/2026/feb/07/norway-royals-epstein-files-court-case))*
- **Listhaug demands independent Epstein investigation, criticizes government response** — FrP leader Sylvi Listhaug demanded full independent investigation of Norwegian connections to Jeffrey Epstein, criticized PM Støre for rejecting the proposal, and called for parliament to force an investigation. Attacked what she described as elite culture in Norwegian diplomacy. *([vg.no](https://www.nettavisen.no/nyheter/store-sier-nei-til-ekstern-gransking-av-ud/s/5-95-2866533))*
- **Listhaug proposes reopening Svalbard coal mines and opposes Melkøya electrification project** — FrP leader Sylvi Listhaug proposed reopening coal mining on Svalbard for security policy reasons and opposed the Melkøya LNG electrification project. SV initially supported stopping the project but reversed position citing legal concerns. *([vg.no](https://www.vg.no/nyheter/i/167o9q/sylvi-listhaug-vil-aapne-nye-kullgruver-paa-svalbard))*
- **Finance Minister Stoltenberg proposes eliminating 1000-kroner banknotes to combat crime** — Finance Minister Jens Stoltenberg proposed eliminating Norway's largest banknote denomination and reducing cash transaction limits from 40,000 to 10,000 kroner as anti-crime measures. Critics argued it would primarily affect honest citizens rather than criminals. *([nettavisen.no](https://www.nationen.no/1000-lappen-det-er-lett-for-ein-toppolitikar-a-forby-ting-som-ikkje-angar-han-sjolv/o/5-148-851950))*
- **Norway's Armed Forces expand training and defense preparations amid heightened threat environment** — Norwegian Armed Forces reported on various activities including expanded conscription, simulator training, Ukraine assistance programs, and enhanced cyber security measures. Defence Minister emphasized this as most serious security situation since WWII. *([forsvaret.no](https://www.nrk.no/innlandet/forsvaret-sparer-enorme-belop-pa-a-bruke-simulatorer-under-ovelser-1.17746708))*
